WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:03.509
Good morning Hank; it’s Monday. Today’s
video is like Voldemort’s soul: It comes

00:00:03.509 --> 00:00:04.799
to you in seven parts.

00:00:04.799 --> 00:00:08.840
Part one: Butler. Hank, my hometown Butler
Bulldogs are in the men’s basketball final

00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:13.870
four for the second consecutive year, an astonishing
achievement that looked like this at the bar

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:14.870
where I watched the game.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:23.050
(Incoherent cheering and screaming, John jostles
the camera a lot and screams.)

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:26.320
I watched all the previous rounds at home,
they mostly looked like this: Matt Howard,

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:33.440
your sister is a Nerdfighter, you can hit
this free throw, you can! YES! (John sits

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:34.440
on Elmo, who says something) You’re ruining
it, Elmo. You’re ruining it.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:38.030
By the way, Hank, Butler star Matt Howard’s
sister Laura really is a Nerdfighter. Sports

00:00:38.030 --> 00:00:40.859
and Nerdfighteria, Hank, the connections are
endless.

00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:45.930
Part two: Brandishing. Hank, on twitter Samisemicolon
writes “I love when at realjohngreen brandishes

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:50.679
random things like black Santas in his videos.”
Brandish. Brandish. Brandish!

00:00:50.679 --> 00:00:53.929
Part three: a definition. Hank, it belatedly
occurs to me that some Nerdfighters might

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:58.010
not even know what basketball is, so let me
define it. Basically there’s two hollow

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:03.100
cylinders and one sphere and you’re trying
to put the sphere in one of the hollow cylinders

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.270
without having it go in the other hollow cylinder.
It’s essentially participatory geometry.

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:12.090
Part four: Finalists. So Hank, this sparkly
little number "Will Grayson, Will Grayson,"

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:16.300
which I co-wrote, is a finalist for the Children’s
Book Choice Award. Hank, actual teenagers

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:20.030
pick the winner of this award, so if you are
an actual teenager go to the link in the doobly-do

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:23.650
and vote for your favorite book of the year.
Also also also I just learned that my new

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:28.570
book is definitely coming out in March! Not
like, in the next two days, but, you know,

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:33.649
we got another March that’s gonna come around.
That one. Ohhh, I should probably settle on

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:34.799
a title soon.

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:38.670
Part five: an imaginary conversation with
Lebron James. Hi Lebron James, so what do

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:43.600
you do for a living? “I insert spheres into
cylinders!” Wow, that sounds extremely tedious

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.540
and probably not very lucrative. “Actually
it’s incredibly fun and I make millions

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.719
of dollars.” Huh. I wouldn’t have thought
that job pays well.

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:55.341
Part six: Titles. So Hank, Nerdfighteria generated
more than two thousand titles for my new book,

00:01:55.341 --> 00:01:58.869
many of which were very good, but none of
which I’m going to use. My original title

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:02.631
idea was to call the book “The Hectic Glow”
after the great Thoreau quote “decay and

00:02:02.631 --> 00:02:06.670
disease are often beautiful, like the pearly
tear of the shellfish or the hectic glow of

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:09.330
consumption”, but the problem with “The
Hectic Glow” is that it’s like the rural

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:14.500
juror of book titles. You say it and people
are like “What? The Metric Snow? The Hectic

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.530
Blow?” I don’t want people to think I
wrote a book called the Hectic Blow! Then

00:02:18.530 --> 00:02:22.070
I thought I might call it “Omnis Cellula
e Cellula” and the nice folks at Penguin

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:26.020
were like, “You know what doesn’t smell
like a bestseller? Latin.” So I still don’t

00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:29.220
have a firm title. If you have any new suggestions,
feel free to leave them in comments.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.170
Part seven: “Sports is like life also poop”
is a great quote from Esther. Anyway, Hank,

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:36.930
I would argue that sports matter. So like
if you’re really into Harry Potter and you

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:40.680
got an email from JK Rowling, that would be
amazing, right? Like imagine that moment.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.600
That would be a great moment. But for some
people those awesome moments are different,

00:02:44.600 --> 00:02:47.650
like the Cubs winning the World Series or
a bunch of unheralded kids from a liberal

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:49.670
arts school in Indiana going to the Final
Four.

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:53.100
Hank, we choose what we care about, and while
there are better and worse choices, I would

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:57.810
argue that caring about sports has nothing
to do with Lebron James or participatory geometry.

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:01.600
Ultimately, it’s about community. I’d
argue that caring about sports is actually

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:05.760
a lot like caring about Harry Potter or Dungeons
and Dragons or Nerdfighteria. Caring about

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.880
stuff binds us to the other people who care
about that stuff, and that creates the communities

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.310
that make life worth living.

00:03:11.310 --> 00:03:14.849
So Hank, that’s my defense of sports. Go
Bulldogs! I’ll see you on Wednesday.

